<worksheet xml:id="sec-chapter1-review">
    <title>Exam 1 Review</title>
    <example>
        <title>Computing an Angle in Radians</title>
        <statement>
        <p>A circle of radius <m>2</m> yards has an angle <m>\theta</m> that intercepts an arc measuring <m>17</m> inches. What is the radian measure of the angle <m>\theta</m>?</p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
        <p>
            <m>\theta\approx 0.1889</m> radians.
        </p>
    </answer>
    </example>
    <example>
        <title>Drawing and Labeling an Angle in Standard Position</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Draw the angle <m>\theta=\dfrac{5\pi}{3}</m> in standard position. Label the initial side, terminal side, and the angle <m>\theta</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <figure xml:id="fig-chapter-1-review-2">
                <caption>An angle of <m>\theta=\dfrac{5\pi}{3}</m> radians drawn in standard position. 
                    The angle is labeled and indicates a counterclockwise rotation.
                    The positive <m>x</m>-axis is labeled as the initial side, and the ray passing through the point <m>\left(\dfrac{1}{2},-\dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\right)</m> is labeled as the terminal side.
                </caption>
                <image source="trigonometry/chapter-1-review-2.png" decorative="yes" width="50%"/>
            </figure>
        </answer>
    </example>
    <example>
        <title>Converting an Angle from Radians to Degrees</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Convert the angle <m>\theta=\dfrac{373\pi}{90}</m> radians to degrees.</p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                <m>\theta=746^\circ</m>
            </p>
        </answer>
    </example>
    <example>
        <title>Converting an Angle from Degrees to Radians</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Convert the angle <m>\theta=-144^\circ</m> to radians.</p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                <m>\theta=-\dfrac{4\pi}{5}</m> radians.
            </p>
        </answer>
    </example>
    <example>
        <title>Finding a Coterminal Angle in Radians</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Find a positive angle <m>\theta</m> that is coterminal to the angle <m>\dfrac{38\pi}{13}</m>, where <m>0\leq \theta\lt 2\pi</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                <m>\theta=\dfrac{12\pi}{13}</m> radians.
            </p>
        </answer>
    </example>
    <example>
        <title>Finding a Coterminal Angle in Degrees</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Find a positive angle <m>\theta</m> that is coterminal to the angle <m>-1100^\circ</m>, where <m>0^\circ\leq \theta\lt 360^\circ</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                <m>\theta=340^\circ</m>.
            </p>
        </answer>
    </example>
    <example>
        <title>Finding the Arc Length and Area of a Sector</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Using the circle given in the figure, find the length of the arc <m>s</m> and the area of the sector formed by the central angle <m>\theta</m>. 
                For both the area and the arc length, give both the exact value and approximate the value to one decimal place.
                Make sure to include the correct units in your answers.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-chapter-1-review-7">
                <caption>A circle with radius <m>r=4</m> cm and central angle <m>\theta=\frac{2\pi}{5}</m>. 
                    <m>\theta</m> is not in standard position; its initial side is in Quadrant 1 and terminal side is in Quadrant 2. 
                    The length of the radius and central angle are labeled with its measurements, and the arc intercepted by <m>\theta</m> is labeled <m>s</m>.</caption>
            <image source="trigonometry/chapter-1-review-7.png" decorative="yes" width="50%"/>
            </figure>
        </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                The length of the arc <m>s</m> is <m>\dfrac{8\pi}{5}\text{ cm} \approx 5.0\text{ cm}</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                The area <m>A</m> of the sector is <m>\dfrac{16\pi}{5}\text{ cm}^2 \approx 10.1\text{ cm}^2</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </answer>
    </example>
    <example>
        <title>Find Linear and Angular Speed</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                The wheels of a certain bicycle has a <m>28\text{ in}</m> diameter. 
                If the wheels are rotating at a constant rate of <m>150\text{ revolutions per minute}</m>, how fast is the bicycle moving in miles per hour?
                Round your answer to <m>2</m> decimal places.
                Include the correct units in your answer.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
            <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Writing all the units out in your calculation will help with the unit conversions needed to solve this problem.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        For example, write <m>150\text{ revolutions per minute}</m> as <m>\dfrac{150\text{ revolutions}}{1\text{ minute}}</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Your goal is to make the the units of measurement in the numerator miles and the denominator hours.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Remember, units cancel in the numerator and denominator just like numbers do!
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
        </hint>
        <answer>
            <p>
                The bicycle is moving at approximately <m>12.49\text{ miles per hour}</m>.
            </p>
        </answer>
    </example>
    <example>
        <title>Find the Six Trigonometric Function using Right Trangles</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Suppose <m>\theta</m> is one of the non-right angles in a right triangle where the side opposite <m>\theta</m> has length <m>7\text {cm}</m> and has a hypotenuse of length <m>13\text{ cm}</m>.
                Find the exact value of the six trigonometric functions of <m>\theta</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                <md>
                    <mrow>\sin(\theta)\amp=\dfrac{7}{13}\amp \csc(\theta)\amp=\dfrac{13}{7}</mrow>
                    <mrow></mrow>
                    <mrow>\cos(\theta)\amp=\dfrac{\sqrt{120}}{13}\amp \sec(\theta)\amp=\dfrac{13}{\sqrt{120}}</mrow>
                    <mrow></mrow>
                    <mrow>\tan(\theta)\amp=\dfrac{7}{\sqrt{120}}\amp \cot(\theta)\amp=\dfrac{\sqrt{120}}{7}</mrow>
                </md>
                Note: <m>\sqrt{120}=2\sqrt{30}</m>, which means the following are also acceptable answers:
                <md>
                    <mrow>\cos(\theta)\amp=\dfrac{2\sqrt{30}}{13}</mrow>
                    <mrow></mrow>
                    <mrow>\sec(\theta)\amp=\dfrac{13}{2\sqrt{30}}=\dfrac{13\sqrt{30}}{60}</mrow>
                    <mrow></mrow>
                    <mrow>\tan(\theta)\amp=\dfrac{7}{2\sqrt{30}}=\dfrac{7\sqrt{30}}{60}</mrow>
                    <mrow></mrow>
                    <mrow>\cot(\theta)\amp=\dfrac{2\sqrt{30}}{7}</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
        </answer>
    </example>
    <example>
        <title>Deriving Pythagorean Identities</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Recall that for any angle <m>\theta</m>, <m>\sin^2(\theta)+\cos^2(\theta)=1</m>. Prove that <m>\tan^2(\theta)+1=\sec^2(\theta)</m> and that <m>1+\cot^2(\theta)=\csc^2(\theta)</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
            <p>
                Divide by either <m>\sin^2(\theta)</m> or <m>\cos^2(\theta)</m> and use quotient identities.
            </p>
        </hint>
    </example>
    <example>
        <title>Use a Calculator to Evaluate a Trigonometric Function in Degrees</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Use a calculator to evaluate <m>\tan(212^\circ)</m>. Round your answer to four decimal places.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                <m>\tan(212^\circ)\approx 0.6428</m>.
            </p>
        </answer>
    </example>
    <example>
        <title>Use a Calculator to Evaluate a Trigonometric Function in Radians</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Use a calculator to evaluate <m>\csc\left(\dfrac{2\pi}{7}\right)</m>. Round your answer to four decimal places.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                <m>\csc\left(\dfrac{2\pi}{7}\right)\approx 1.2790</m>.
            </p>
        </answer>
    </example>
    <example>
        <title>Applications using Right Triangles</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                The ramp of a moving truck touches the ground <m>14\text{ ft}</m> away from the end of the truck. 
                If the ramp makes an angle of <m>25^\circ</m> with the ground, how long is the ramp?
                Round your answer to two decimal places and include the correct units in your answer.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                The ramp is approximately <m>15.45\text{ ft}</m> long.
            </p>
        </answer>
    </example>
    <example>
        <title>Applications using Right Triangles</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                A telephone pole is <m>75\text{ ft}</m> tall. A guy wire <m>100\text{ ft}</m> long is attached from the ground to the top of the pole. Find the angle between the wire and the pole to the nearest degree.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                The angle between the wire and the pole is approximately <m>41^\circ</m>.
            </p>
        </answer>
    </example>
    <example>
        <title>Using Reference Angles to Evaluate Trigonometric Functions</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Suppose the point <m>(-120,209)</m> is a point on the terminal side of an angle <m>\theta</m> in standard position. 
                Draw the reference triangle for this point, label the sides and the reference angle, and find the exact value of the six trigonometric functions of <m>\theta</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
                <figure xml:id="fig-chapter-1-review-17">
                    <caption>A reference triangle for the point <m>(-120,209)</m>. 
                        The angle <m>\theta</m> is in standard position, and the reference angle <m>\theta^{\,\prime}</m> is labeled as the angle between the hypotenuse and the negative x-axis.
                        The triangle is a right triangle with one vertex at the origin, one vertex at the point <m>(-120,209)</m>, and the last vertex on the point <m>(-120,0)</m>. 
                        The side adjacent to <m>\theta^{\,\prime}</m> has length <m>-120</m>, the side opposite <m>\theta^{\,\prime}</m> has length <m>209</m>, and the hypotenuse has length <m>241</m>. 
                        </caption>
                        <image source="trigonometry/chapter-1-review-17.png" decorative="yes" width="50%"/>
                </figure>
                <p>
                The six trigonometric functions of <m>\theta</m> are calculated below.
                <md>
                    <mrow>\sin(\theta)\amp=\dfrac{209}{241}\amp \csc(\theta)\amp=\dfrac{241}{209}</mrow>
                    <mrow></mrow>
                    <mrow>\cos(\theta)\amp=-\dfrac{120}{241}\amp \sec(\theta)\amp=-\dfrac{241}{120}</mrow>
                    <mrow></mrow>
                    <mrow>\tan(\theta)\amp=-\dfrac{209}{120}\amp \cot(\theta)\amp=-\dfrac{120}{209}</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
        </answer>
    </example>
    <example xml:id="exa-quadrants-review">
        <title>Finding the Quadrant of an Angle</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                If <m>\sin(\theta)\lt 0</m> and <m>\cos(\theta)\gt 0</m>, in which quadrant does the angle <m>\theta</m> lie?
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                The angle <m>\theta</m> lies in Quadrant 4.
            </p>
        </answer>
    </example>
    <example xml:id="exa-quadrants-review-2">
        <title>Finding the Quadrant of an Angle</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                If <m>\cos(\theta)=-\dfrac{2}{3}</m> and <m>\tan(\theta)\gt 0</m>, in which quadrant does the angle <m>\theta</m> lie?
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                The angle <m>\theta</m> lies in Quadrant 3.
            </p>
        </answer>
    </example>
    <example xml:id="exa-negative-angle-review">
        <title>Finding the value of a Trigonometric Function of a Negative Angle</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find the exact value of <m>\sin(-135^\circ)</m> using the fact that sine is an odd function. 
                You may have to use additional techniques in addition to the oddness of sine to get a final answer.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                <m>\sin(-135^\circ)=-\dfrac{\sqrt{2}}{2}</m>.
            </p>
        </answer>
    </example>
    <example xml:id="exa-periodicity-review">
        <title>Use the Periodicity of Sine or Cosine to Evaluate a Trigonometric Function</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Use the periodicity of cosine to evaluate <m>\cos\left(\dfrac{19\pi}{3}\right)</m>. 
                You may have to use additional techniques in addition to the periodicity of cosine to get a final answer.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                <m>\cos\left(\dfrac{19\pi}{3}\right)=\dfrac{1}{2}</m>.
            </p>
        </answer>
    </example>
    <example xml:id="exa-transformation-sine-graph-review">
        <title>Graph a Transformation of a Sine Function</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Graph one period of the function <m>y=3\sin\left(2x-\dfrac{\pi}{4}\right)+1</m>.
                Identify the amplitude, period, phase shift, and vertical shift of the function.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>\text{Amplitude} =3</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>\text{Period} =\pi</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>\text{Phase Shift} = \dfrac{\pi}{8}</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>\text{Vertical Shift} = 1</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-chapter-1-review-transformation-sine-graph">
                <caption>The graph of <m>y=3\sin\left(2x-\frac{\pi}{4}\right)+1</m> plotted on <m>\left[-\frac{\pi}{4},\frac{11\pi}{8}\right]\times\left[-3,\frac{11}{2}\right]</m>.
                A line segment measuring the distance from <m>\left(\frac{\pi}{8},1\right)</m> to <m>\left(\frac{9\pi}{8},1\right)</m> is labeled, "One period of <m>y=3\sin\left(2x-\frac{\pi}{4}\right)+1</m>."</caption>
                <image source="trigonometry/chapter-1-review-transformation-sine-graph.png" decorative="yes"/>
            </figure>
        </answer>
    </example>
    <example xml:id="exa-transformation-cosine-graph-review">
        <title>Graph a Transformation of a Cosine Function</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Graph one period of the function <m>y=-2\cos\left(\dfrac{\pi}{2}x\right)</m>.
                Identify the amplitude, period, phase shift, and vertical shift of the function.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>\text{Amplitude} =2</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>\text{Period} =4\pi</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>\text{Phase Shift} = 0</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>\text{Vertical Shift} = 0</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-chapter-1-review-transformation-cosine-graph">
                <caption>The graph of <m>y=-2\cos\left(\frac{\pi}{2}x\right)</m> plotted on <m>\left[-2,6\right]\times\left[-3,4\right]</m>.
                A line segment measuring the distance from <m>(0,0)</m> to <m>(4,0)</m> is labeled, "One period of <m>y=-2\cos\left(\frac{\pi}{2}x\right)</m>."</caption>
                <image source="trigonometry/chapter-1-review-transformation-cosine-graph.png" decorative="yes"/>
            </figure>
        </answer>
    </example>
</worksheet>